---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/privy';
const title = 'Privy for Shopify: connect capture, offers and messaging';
const desc = 'Evaluate Privy availability, email and SMS scope, Sendlane account continuity, storefront offers and usable signup economics.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify operations · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy for Shopify: connect capture, offers and messaging</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Privy combines onsite list growth with email and SMS marketing. Flexible popups and targeted offers can help a store turn a landing-page visit into a usable subscriber relationship, while messaging can continue the journey. Evaluate the supported account and installation route first, then prove the offer and identity path. This review does not establish a merchant conversion lift or require a second sender.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Availability and account scope come first</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">Privy Shopify listing</a> currently says the app is unavailable and directs support questions to Privy Operations. This limits what can be inferred about installing from that listing. It does not prove that Privy has closed or that existing connected accounts stopped working. Confirm the supported Shopify installation or reconnection route directly before planning a launch.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/">current Privy site</a> describes popups, email/SMS campaigns, segmentation, reporting and a visual automation builder from welcome through winback. Calling it only a capture layer with no lifecycle capability would misrepresent that scope. Marketing pages establish product positioning, not that every legacy account has the same builder, integrations or entitlements.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">February 2026 acquisition announcement</a> says Privy acquired Sendlane and describes continued investment for Privy customers. It does not establish an automatic migration date or feature parity for a particular Sendlane account. Existing users should confirm login, billing, support ownership, active automations, sender domains, SMS numbers and any required reconnection before changing production settings.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Follow the offer from display to an eligible send</h2>
      <p>For collection traffic, define a relevant prompt and display surface before choosing an incentive. Compare a category guide with a discount for informational visitors; use product-specific creative for a jewelry landing page. Validate mobile behavior separately from desktop exit intent. Suppress redundant offers for known subscribers or purchasers where the account supports the required rule.</p>
      <p>Prepare a field contract for email, normalized phone, source, selected interest, offer identifier and permission evidence. Verify whether each field is stored in Privy, written to Shopify or passed to an explicitly supported destination. Do not assume arbitrary quiz answers become Shopify tags or that a named external sender has a working native integration.</p>
      <p>Test a fresh visitor, existing email subscriber, email-only contact adding SMS, withdrawn contact and customer correcting an address. Inspect profile updates and flow entries; a successful form submission is different from a valid subscription and an eligible send. Repeat the submission and retry a failed sync to check that the same welcome or coupon is not issued twice.</p>
      <p>If Privy sends the welcome, assign it ownership of that step. If another sender is retained, validate its connector, field mapping and current suppression before enabling the handoff. Stop duplicate welcome and cart queues. A purchase after signup should cancel an obsolete acquisition incentive; no universal twenty-four-hour handoff or channel-spacing rule follows.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed storefront and lifecycle exercises</h2>
      <p>These are acceptance and experiment designs, not account tests or observed merchant outcomes. Confirm the display, trigger and filtering controls in the actual account before promising the experience.</p>
      <p><strong>Jewelry welcome:</strong> propose a 10% offer on a $240 necklace page. Check code generation, expiry, eligibility, reuse and discount combinations before enabling a spin or popup experience. The face discount is $24 and the pre-tax price after discount is $216. Verify product links and stock in the welcome. Compare the offer with a suitable alternative in randomized eligible traffic; no 19% redemption or 34% subscriber growth was observed.</p>
      <p><strong>Mobile cart:</strong> propose a cart-saver prompt for a $65 basket and an eligible follow-up. Test cart changes, empty baskets, checkout completion and return visits before measuring recovery. Recheck channel permission and purchase state at dispatch, cancel stale recovery and deduplicate retries. Separate onsite purchases from follow-up attribution and report net contribution per assigned shopper.</p>
      <p><strong>Holiday gift order:</strong> propose a thank-you with care instructions and a separately eligible later promotion. Validate the source and availability of gift flags, recipient versus purchaser identity, fulfillment and refund state. A service message or gift purchase does not establish promotional permission. Test the actual post-purchase filters rather than asserting that gift exclusions are absent.</p>
      <p><strong>Lapsed buyers:</strong> propose 1,200 one-time buyers without a purchase in 90 days. Compare useful full-price content with a 15% incentive among eligible randomized groups and a holdout where feasible. Remove recent purchasers and withdrawn contacts before dispatch. Include returns, discount costs and uncertainty; no 2.8% versus 5.1% supplier result or revenue loss is established.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Use workload inputs and usable signup economics</h2>
      <p>Get a dated quote for the supported account: 2,500 contacts with an 80,000-pageview month, a 400,000-pageview traffic spike and a separate 12,000-contact workload with 25,000 email sends. Ask which contacts and activity are billable, what sending limits apply, how overages behave and what SMS destinations, message segments, number costs and carrier fees add. These are quote inputs, not verified pageview pricing or $30-to-$90 tier changes.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical 80,000 eligible popup impressions in one month, a 4.2% signup rate produces 3,360 submissions in that month. Deduplicate people and exclude unusable or unpermissioned records before treating this as subscriber growth. Pageviews are not automatically eligible impressions, and this calculation is not a quarterly result.</p>
      <p>Cost per usable new signup is capture and acquisition cost divided by new eligible contacts successfully recorded. If hypothetical allocated cost is $1,400 and 1,000 usable new contacts arrive, the ratio is $1.40. It says nothing by itself about profit. Compare contribution after offers, returns, messaging and labor over a defined purchase window, with sample size and uncertainty.</p>
      <p>Budget creative production, account-transition work, connector maintenance and failed-send investigation alongside subscription costs. Extra lifecycle software is justified only by a verified workflow need and matched all-in economics. Revenue or order count alone does not create a mandatory upgrade threshold or prove that Privy-only operation loses $2,000 per month.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Keep account continuity separate from capture experiments</h2>
      <p>For an influencer landing page, test headline and incentive independently and retain source metadata without overwriting an explicit product preference. For organic educational traffic, compare a guide with an immediate offer. For returning buyers, inspect actual recognition and frequency rules; a stored interest or old purchase tag is not permanent eligibility.</p>
      <p>Existing Privy or Sendlane operators should inventory live displays, coupons, campaigns, automations, suppressions and pending queues before any transition. Confirm whether historical events, consent evidence and reporting remain accessible. Validate domain authentication and SMS identity with the supplier, then use controlled contacts and orders to verify the replacement path. Keep a rollback owner and avoid replaying historical events as new welcome entries.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="/apps/justuno">Justuno review</a> follows capture into a separate destination; Privy also describes its own messaging. The <a class="link" href="/apps/marsello">Marsello review</a> follows membership and reward redemption across POS and online. Compare these actual jobs instead of assigning a blanket winner for retention. Existing <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">comparison pages</a> are further reading, not evidence that their supplier claims were revalidated here.</p>
      <p>Preserve purpose, channel and destination-specific permission evidence and withdrawals. Recheck suppression and current subscription state at send time; email signup does not authorize promotional SMS. Assign reply and failed-handoff ownership and record duplicate entries and stale queues alongside signup totals. Maintain the simplest supported capture and messaging path that passes the store’s acceptance cases.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
